Identifiers in JavaLast Updated : 17 Mar 2025In Java programming, identifiers help make different elements inside a program easier to recognize and use by acting as symbolic names for them. Numerous entities, including classes, variables, methods, packages, constants, and more, can be represented by these identifiers. Developers can enhance the readability and maintainability of code by giving these elements meaningful names.In Java programming, identifiers are essential because they let programmers efficiently reference and modify program parts. A class identifier uniquely identifies a class definition to enable the production of objects and the calling of class methods, for example. In a similar vein, method IDs designate executable code blocks, and variable identifiers stand in for storage locations connected to data values.Identifiers in Java are symbolic names used for identification. It can be a class name, variable name, method name, package name, constant name, and other names used in programming. However, in Java, some reserved words cannot be used as an identifier.For every identifier, some conventions should be used before declaring them. Let's understand it with a simple Java program:public class HelloJava {
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		System.out.println("Hello JavaTpoint");
	}
}
In the above example, we have used the following identifiers:HelloJava (Class name)main (main method)String (Predefined Class name)args (String variables)System (Predefined class)out (Variable name)println (method)Importance of IdentifiersProgramme Structure: Classes, variables, methods, packages, and other things are named by identifiers that are the fundamental building blocks of Java programmes.Readability and Understanding: When well-chosen identifiers improve the readability and comprehensibility of code, developers can more easily comprehend the function and goal of various program components.Modifiability: By offering a simple method of referencing and altering program elements, identifiers aid in code maintenance and change.Communication: Identifiers allow developers to communicate with each other by sharing details about the purpose and capabilities of certain program pieces.Let's understand the rules for Java identifiers.Rules for Java IdentifiersThere are some rules and conventions for declaring the identifiers in Java. If the identifiers are not properly declared, we may get a compile-time error. Following are some rules and conventions for declaring identifiers:A valid identifier must have characters [A-Z] or [a-z] or numbers [0-9], and underscore (_) or a dollar sign ($). For example, @javatpoint is not a valid identifier because it contains a special character @.There should not be any space in an identifier. For example, java tpoint is an invalid identifier.An identifier should not contain a number at the starting. For example, 123javatpoint is an invalid identifier.An identifier should be of length 4-15 letters only. However, there is no limit on its length. But, it is good to follow the standard conventions.We cannot use the Java reserved keywords as an identifier such as int, float, double, char, etc. For example, int double is an invalid identifier in Java.An identifier should not be any query language keywords such as SELECT, FROM, COUNT, DELETE, etc.Naming ConventionsClasses: Class names should start with an uppercase letter and follow the CamelCase convention. For example, MyClass.Variables: Variable names should start with a lowercase letter and follow the camelCase convention. For example, myVariable.Methods: Method names should start with a lowercase letter and follow the camelCase convention. For example, myMethod.Constants: Constants should be in all uppercase letters with words separated by underscores. For example, PI_VALUE.Packages: Package names should be in lowercase letters and follow a reverse domain name convention. For example, com.example.package.Java Reserved KeywordsJava reserved keywords are predefined words, which are reserved for any functionality or meaning. We cannot use these keywords as our identifier names, such as class name or method name. These keywords are used by the syntax of Java for some functionality. If we use a reserved word as our variable name, it will throw an error.In Java, every reserved word has a unique meaning and functionality.Consider the following syntax:double marks;
In the above statement, double is a reserved word while marks is a valid identifier.The following table represents the reserved keywords in Java:abstractcontinueforprotectedtransientassertdefaultgotopublictrybooleandoifstaticthrowsbreakdoubleimplementsstrictfppackagebyteelseimportsuperprivatecaseenuminterfaceshortswitchcatchextendsinstanceOfreturnvoidcharfinalintsynchronizedvolatileclassfinallylongthrowdateconstfloatnativethiswhileAlthough the const and goto are not part of the Java language; But, they are also considered keywords.Example of Valid and Invalid IdentifiersValid identifiers:Following are some examples of valid identifiers in Java:TestVariabletestvariableaiTest_Variable_testvariable$testvariablesum_of_arrayTESTVARIABLEjtp123JavaTpointJavatpoint123Invalid identifiersFollowing are some examples of invalid identifiers:Test Variable (We cannot include a space in an identifier)123javatpoint (The identifier should not begin with numbers)java+tpoint (The plus (+) symbol cannot be used)a-javatpoint (Hyphen symbol is not allowed)java_&_Tpoint (ampersand symbol is not allowed)Java'tpoint (we cannot use an apostrophe symbol in an identifier)We should follow some naming convention while declaring an identifier. However, these conventions are not forced to follow by the Java programming language. That's why it is called conventions, not rules. But it is good to follow them. These are some industry standards and recommended by Java communities such as Oracle and Netscape.If we do not follow these conventions, it may generate confusion or erroneous code.Role of Naming ConventionsStandardization: Naming conventions establish standard practices for naming identifiers, promoting consistency and uniformity across codebases.Clarity and Readability: By following naming conventions, developers can enhance the clarity and readability of code, making it easier to understand and maintain.Community Standards: Naming conventions are often based on industry best practices and community standards, ensuring that code is written in a manner familiar to other developers.Filename: IdentifierExample.javapublic class IdentifierExample {

    // Constants should be in all uppercase letters with words separated by underscores

    public static final double PI_VALUE = 3.14159;

    // Method names should start with a lowercase letter and follow camelCase convention

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Variable names should start with a lowercase letter and follow camelCase convention

        int myVariable = 5;

        // Object creation follows the same conventions as variable names

        IdentifierExample exampleObject = new IdentifierExample();

        // Method invocation also follows the same conventions

        exampleObject.printMessage("Hello, world!");

        // Constant usage with appropriate naming convention

        System.out.println("Value of PI: " + PI_VALUE);
    }

    // Method names should start with a lowercase letter and follow camelCase convention

    public void printMessage(String message) {

        System.out.println(message);
    }
}
Output:Hello, world!
Value of PI: 3.14159
ExplanationThe CamelCase style for class names is demonstrated by the class IdentifierExample. Within it, the constant PI_VALUE is stated using all uppercase letters and underscores to separate words in accordance with constant conventions.The program's entry point, the main method, is declared with the necessary signature for Java programmes. MyVariable, a variable named after the camelCase convention, is declared inside main. The generation of objects for IdentifierExample adheres to the same convention as variable names; an instance of IdentifierExample is represented by exampleObject.The camelCase convention is also used when calling a method, as exampleObject.printMessage("Hello, world!") demonstrates.Lastly, the programme follows the norm for constant usage and outputs the value of the constant PI_VALUE using System.out.println().ConclusionTo sum up, Java identifiers act as symbolic names for a variety of programme components, such as variables, classes, methods, packages, constants, and more. They are essential in making it easier to identify and use these components in a Java programme. Code readability, maintainability, and teamwork can all be improved by developers by following standard naming conventions and best practices.We have covered the importance of identifiers in Java programming throughout this talk, highlighting how they help organise code, enhance understanding, and facilitate productive developer communication. We've also covered the guidelines and customs surrounding the naming of identifiers, emphasising the significance of choosing consistent, meaningful names while staying away from reserved terms and following accepted naming practices.Next TopicRulebasedcollator-getcollationelementiterator-string-method-in-java ← prev next → | abstract | continue | for | protected | transient | assert | default | goto | public | try | boolean | do | if | static | throws | break | double | implements | strictfp | package | byte | else | import | super | p